# Detect Code Smells

Use the `code-smell-detector` subagent to scan for code duplication, refactoring opportunities, and dead code. The subagent is **readonly** — it reports findings and suggests fixes but does NOT make changes. The user decides what to act on.

This rule is **not automatic on every change**. Use the `code-smell-detector` occasionally when it seems appropriate — e.g., after significant code changes, large refactors, or adding new classes. Use your judgment; it does not need to run on every edit.

## What It Detects

### Code Duplication

- Repeated logic blocks (3+ similar occurrences)
- Copy-pasted code with minor variations
- Similar conditional structures across methods or classes
- Repeated patterns that could be parameterized into a shared helper

### Refactoring Opportunities

- Long methods that do too many things (consider splitting)
- Deep nesting (3+ levels of if/for/try) that could be flattened
- God classes with too many responsibilities
- Feature envy (method uses another class's data more than its own)
- Shotgun surgery patterns (one change requires editing many places)

### Dead Code

- Unreachable code after return/raise/break/continue
- Unused private methods or functions
- Unused variables or assignments
- Commented-out code blocks
- Unused class attributes
- Unused imports (also covered by `code-style-enforcer`)

## Subagent

For comprehensive detection, use the `code-smell-detector` subagent. It is readonly and will report findings with suggested fixes — the user decides what to act on.
